id: pm-htbetn
title: "Lifecycle field-completeness contract: each item type declares the fields required at each status transition, machine-checked by pm validate"
description: "Best-practice PM structure exists only by convention: Epics carry charters, Stories carry persona-goal-benefit, Decisions carry context/options/consequences, Features carry acceptance criteria, closed items carry resolution evidence — but pm validate checks only close-time resolution fields (pm-wenq) and nothing else. Define a per-type, per-transition field-completeness contract as schema data (not hardcoded): type X moving to status Y requires/expects fields Z, with warn vs require per governance policy. Validate gains a --check-completeness class reporting per-type violations; CI rides the pm-kpftft ratchet so completeness only improves. Schema customization (pm-8jdc) lets projects extend the contract for custom types; the GA contract (pm-xp1xsw) publishes the built-in defaults. This is what turns 'well-defined pm items' from a social norm into a checked invariant, and it gives the context engine (pm-4k6b) a machine-readable knowledge-density signal."
type: Feature
status: closed
priority: 2
tags[6]: context-management,data-quality,governance,schema,standards,validation
created_at: "2026-07-26T10:01:01.886Z"
updated_at: "2026-09-08T21:57:59.136Z"
closed_at: "2026-09-08T21:57:58.519Z"
completed_at: "2026-09-08T21:57:58.519Z"
author: "harness:opencode"
estimated_minutes: 600
acceptance_criteria: Contract declared as schema data with built-in defaults per type; pm validate reports per-type completeness violations; warn/require follows governance policy; custom types extend via schema; negative-control fixture in CI proves a malformed item fails
goal: project management = context management
objective: "Make item well-formedness a declared, machine-checked contract per type and lifecycle stage"
value: Agents can trust that any Epic has a charter and any Decision has consequences; completeness becomes a scoring signal instead of a hope
why_now: "2026-07-26 review found close-time completeness (pm-wenq) tracked but the general per-type, per-transition contract absent; 287 closed items already fail today's narrow resolution check, showing conventions erode without gates"
parent: pm-33cw
risk: low
confidence: high
resolution: "Delivered customizable per-type lifecycle completeness rules, optional built-in presets and counts-first validation. Each corpus scan reuses one private policy snapshot; unrelated history diagnostics cannot falsely invalidate item-source completeness."
expected_result: "Schema-defined lifecycle requirements are validated consistently for custom item types and statuses, with bounded per-type diagnostics and no repeated policy hashing per item."
actual_result: "Linked completeness and author-attribution checks pass; snapshot mutation isolation and fresh-evaluator behavior pass. Sixth-round verification: all 8622 tests in 653 files pass with exact 100/100/100/100 coverage and zero uncovered statements, branches, functions and lines. All four TypeScript projects, the full quality:static gate, serialized docs/skills checks, and every linked test command pass. Both original clone profiles remain unchanged; the supplementary historical-table profile also passes. Final pushed-head CI, full platform matrix, review and preflight remain required before merge."
